    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me focus on the technical aspects of the pitot tube.\n\nThe pitot tube works by using the principle of Bernoulli's equation, which states that the pressure of a fluid (in this case, air) decreases as its velocity increases. The pitot tube is designed to capture the air flowing over the nose of the aircraft, which is moving at a high velocity due to the aircraft's forward motion. This creates a pressure difference between the pitot tube and the static ports, which are usually located on the sides of the aircraft.\n\nThe static ports are essentially small holes that allow air to flow into the aircraft, but they are not exposed to the same high-velocity air as the pitot tube. As a result, the air flowing through the static ports has a lower velocity and pressure than the air flowing through the pitot tube. The pressure difference between the two is then measured by the pressure sensor, and the resulting airspeed reading is displayed.\n\nIn a typical aircraft, the pitot tube is connected to a pitot-static system, which includes the static ports and a pressure sensor. The system is designed to provide a reliable and accurate airspeed measurement, even in a",
      "source": "target",
